  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 405 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over western Manitoba with\ncold front dropping down through western North Dakota into\nnorthwest South Dakota, while high settles through Alberta into\nwestern/central Montana. Upper level analysis places low over\nColorado, with northern stream short wave now pushing through \nSaskatchewan nudging into the Northern Plains. \n\nFor today, aforementioned front will continue its progression\nthrough the state with winds increasing in its wake given the\npressure gradient and cold air advection. Forecast soundings\ncontinue to suggest that ongoing mention of gusts to 55 mph\nin the Wind Advisory remain reasonable across the area. The \nonly change to the headline was to extend the expiration of the James\nRiver Valley and parts of south central North Dakota into the \novernight hours as winds look to be slow to diminish over that\narea. Along with the wind, expect to see a few light rain \nshowers in the wake of the front with the passing short wave. \nExpect this could lead to some brief enhancement of wind gusts \nas light showers pass through.\n\nAs we go through tonight, high slides into western North Dakota\nand skies clear, which will allow temperatures to drop off to \nbelow freezing. The question is how far east the skies will \nclear out over central North dakota allowing temperatures to \ndrop off. Therefore, will hold off on any freeze headlines for \nnow until we can get another look at this later today. \n\nOn Friday, surface high slides off to the south/east, while \nupper ridging to the west slides easterly. This will allow \ntemperatures to rebound, though highs will still be a bit below\naverage.\n\nUpper ridge continues its easterly progression going into\nSaturday, when temperatures will climb well above average, with\nmid 60s to mid 70s through the area. Seasonably mild \ntemperatures and dry conditions continue into the first part of\nthe upcoming week.",
